title: 'eth-rpc: fail readiness while catching up and liveness once stalled'
doc:
- audience: [Node Dev, Node Operator]
  description: |-
    An eth-rpc instance that is catching up now reports `isSyncing: true` in `system_health`, so
    it fails `/health/readiness` but not `/health`. An instance is catching up while it is more
    than 5 blocks behind its node. Previously `/health` and `/health/readiness` both failed once
    eth-rpc was more than one block behind.

    Deployments that use `/health/readiness` for the readiness probe and `/health` for the liveness
    probe need no change. Deployments whose readiness probe uses `/health` should switch it to
    `/health/readiness`, or an instance that is catching up keeps receiving traffic.

    `/health` fails once the best block of an instance that is catching up has stayed unchanged
    for more than 60 seconds.

    The health check has three known limitations, which deployments should take into account.
    - When an instance advances but never gets within 5 blocks of its node, it continues to report
      `isSyncing: true` and to fail `/health/readiness`, while `/health` succeeds. Before this
      change `/health` failed as well. A readiness probe that keeps failing should trigger an
      alert.
    - When eth-rpc is connected to a node without boot nodes, `/health/readiness` succeeds even
      while eth-rpc is catching up. Before this change it failed once eth-rpc was more than one
      block behind.
    - When eth-rpc is connected to a node in major sync, the node does not deliver new blocks, so
      `/health` fails once the sync has lasted more than 60 seconds. Before this change it failed
      once eth-rpc was more than one block behind.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: patch
